Mountain Peak by Alexander Alekseev aka TDM (2014)
https://www.shadertoy.com/view/llK3WR

Source of this port: the libretro GLSL conversion of the shader,
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/libretro/glsl-shaders/master/procedural/tdm-mountain-peak.glsl
(libretro/glsl-shaders has no licence file of its own; the licence is stated in
the shader's own header, as below.)

The author's notice in the shader (line 113 of that file, directly under the
line '// "Mountain Peak" by Alexander Alekseev aka TDM - 2014') reads:

    // License Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 3.0 Unported License.

The shader's own comment also credits Inigo Quilez ("thanks to iq") for the
technique of its noise-with-analytical-derivatives function. iquilezles.org says
its code snippets are under the MIT licence; the function here is the author's
own writing of that technique and is kept as he wrote it.

What was changed for this port (also listed at the top of main.metal):
  - The libretro wrapper (version and compatibility defines, vertex stage,
    RETRO_PIXEL_SIZE parameter line, uniforms, main()) was removed.
  - Time: libretro's frame-counter "iGlobalTime" was replaced by iTime seconds
    multiplied by TIME_SCALE (0.3), so the camera drifts slowly round the peak
    (0.03 rad per second) as befits a desktop background.
  - Mouse input was replaced by a constant (the original's no-mouse branch).
  - floatBitsToUint / uintBitsToFloat became as_type<uint> / as_type<float>.
  - Output parameters of hftracing() became thread references; locals that
    GLSL would have zeroed are initialised.
  - "saturate" was renamed sat() (it is a Metal built-in); the unused PI and
    EPSILON constants were dropped.
  Terrain, lighting, fog, wind and tone-mapping are unchanged from the copy this
  port was made from, the one in libretro/glsl-shaders. That copy is an earlier
  revision than the shader now on Shadertoy, which uses more steps and octaves
  and a different tone map.
  scene.json draws it at a reduced resolution and frame rate to keep it cheap.

